.SH NAME
boottarget \- a logical object definition in the xCAT database.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IX Header "SYNOPSIS"
\&\fBboottarget Attributes:\fR  \fIbprofile\fR, \fIcomments\fR, \fIinitrd\fR, \fIkcmdline\fR, \fIkernel\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.IX Header "DESCRIPTION"
Logical objects of this type are stored in the xCAT database in one or more tables.  Use the following commands
to manipulate the objects: \fBmkdef\fR, \fBchdef\fR, \fBlsdef\fR, and \fBrmdef\fR.  These commands will take care of
knowing which tables the object attributes should be stored in.  The attribute list below shows, in
parentheses, what tables each attribute is stored in.
.SH "boottarget Attributes:"
.IX Header "boottarget Attributes:"
.IP "\fBbprofile\fR (boottarget.bprofile)" 6
.IX Item "bprofile (boottarget.bprofile)"
All nodes with a nodetype.profile value equal to this value and nodetype.os set to "boottarget", will use the associated kernel, initrd, and kcmdline.
.IP "\fBcomments\fR (boottarget.comments)" 6
.IX Item "comments (boottarget.comments)"
Any user-written notes.
.IP "\fBinitrd\fR (boottarget.initrd)" 6
.IX Item "initrd (boottarget.initrd)"
The initial ramdisk image that network boot actions should use (could be a DOS floppy or hard drive image if using memdisk as kernel)
.IP "\fBkcmdline\fR (boottarget.kcmdline)" 6
.IX Item "kcmdline (boottarget.kcmdline)"
Arguments to be passed to the kernel
.IP "\fBkernel\fR (boottarget.kernel)" 6
.IX Item "kernel (boottarget.kernel)"
The kernel that network boot actions should currently acquire and use.  Note this could be a chained boot loader such as memdisk or a non-linux boot loader
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.IX Header "SEE ALSO"
\&\fBmkdef\|(1)\fR, \fBchdef\|(1)\fR, \fBlsdef\|(1)\fR, \fBrmdef\|(1)\fR
